# The draft CSV fixtures encode exact byte-level encodings -- BOM presence, delimiter, and
# line terminators, including CRLF *inside* quoted cells. That is their entire purpose: they
# reproduce what Excel, LibreOffice and Google Sheets actually write, so
# tests/integration/test_spreadsheet_roundtrip.py can assert SC-012 against real spreadsheet
# output rather than against files the tool wrote itself.
#
# Git's EOL normalization destroys them. With core.autocrlf=true (the Git for Windows
# default) and no rule here, every CRLF is stripped to LF on commit and every LF rewritten to
# CRLF on checkout -- which silently converts the bare-LF Google Sheets fixture into a CRLF
# one on Windows, and strips the CRLF from the other three on any platform that does not
# smudge them back. The tests then assert against bytes no spreadsheet ever produced.
#
# -text disables all EOL conversion in both directions. Do not remove it, and do not
# "normalize line endings" across these files.
#
# Order matters here: a later line wins for the same attribute, so the broad `text=auto`
# rule must come FIRST and the fixture exemption LAST. Reversing them silently
# re-enables normalization and puts the corrupted fixtures back.

# Default: let Git normalize in the repository and check out native endings.
* text=auto

# Exemption, and it must stay last. See the note above.
tests/fixtures/*.csv -text
